# ============================================================
# check:design — declared-frozen violations (D159)
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. Every entry below is a real GUIDELINES.md violation in
# src/app/housekeeping/my-tasks/MyTasksScreen.tsx. They are NOT forgiven and NOT
# exempt: they are recorded because the module they live in is frozen by owner
# decision in STATE.md —
#
#     "מודול ניקיון/משימות מוקפא לפי החלטת הבעלים — ממתין לאיפיון UI מהבעלים;
#      לא לגעת עד הודעה חדשה."
#
# — and all six were born with the file (b82eac2, 2026-07-21) while the guard was
# already live, so they are original debt, not a regression. Fixing them means
# editing a frozen module; leaving the suite permanently red means the guard stops
# being a signal. This file is the third option: the debt is written down, named,
# and counted, and everything else stays enforced. See DECISIONS.md D159.
#
# THE RULE FOR THIS FILE. No entry may be ADDED without an owner decision — a new
# entry is a new licence to violate the design system, which is exactly what the
# guard exists to prevent. Entries may only be REMOVED, as the violations are
# fixed. The check enforces the ratchet in both directions:
#
#   * an entry that matches nothing FAILS the run ("stale baseline entry")
#   * a violation with no entry FAILS the run, including a NEW violation of the
#     same rule in the same file — one entry suppresses exactly ONE instance
#
# There is deliberately NO way for a run to write to this file: no flag, no
# regeneration path, no --update-baseline. It is hand-edited or it is not edited.
#
# EXIT PLAN. When the owner respecifies the housekeeping UI, the violations are
# fixed with that work and their lines are deleted from here. When the last line
# goes, delete the file — the check treats a missing baseline as an empty one.
#
# FORMAT — one violation instance per line, four fields:
#
#     <rule> | <file> | <detail> | <source line, comments stripped, spaces collapsed>
#
# The fourth field is what makes an entry identify ONE instance rather than a
# whole file+rule bucket: the two text-base violations below are told apart by it
# and by nothing else. It is matched on content, never on line number, so the
# baseline survives unrelated shifts in the file and goes stale the moment the
# offending line itself is edited.
# ============================================================

